From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 14:23:27 -0500
Subject: feat: bring Arc's routes across in the import

Arc keeps its routes in StorableLinkRouting.json, beside the sidebar file. The
import reads it from a copy on the pool, as it reads the sidebar. A rule whose
destination names one Arc Space becomes a route to the Space that Space became,
one route for each of its sources, in the file's order: "contains" keeps its
text, and "matches" is a host rule for a bare host and an exact address for
anything else (import I48).

What a route cannot say stays out and is counted: a rule for a little window, for
the Space used last or a profile's last, one whose Space did not come across, and
a source of another kind. The summary counts the routes before anything is
applied, the result line says how many came and how many stayed out, and the page
after the import says the same and why (import I49). Arc's default for links from
other apps does not come across: little windows are on by default, and the Space
for links from other apps stays the user's.

A second import adds nothing: a route is remembered by its source's id in
stedding.import.arc_ids, so one the user removed stays removed, and one already
here, by kind and text, keeps its Space.

The shape was taken from Arc's own type metadata, which names the cases. A rule
naming a Space and a "matches" source are read as those types declare them; no
real file holding either has been seen. The tests use a made-up file.

Why: a person moving from Arc loses their routes otherwise (BACKLOG S-422).

Removable when: Arc's files are gone from people's computers.
